WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:03.200
- [Narrator] Mariachi, defined
by its blaring trumpets,

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.266
the rumbling guitarrón, the
singers' inimitable grito.

00:00:06.266 --> 00:00:07.290
(singer vocalizing)

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.093
And the iconic mariachi suits
known as trajes de charro.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.690
And to find the best
tailor of these trajes,

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.270
you have to go east of the LA river,

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.170
tucked between the 101 and the 5

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.345
in this unassuming
building in Boyle Heights,

00:00:19.345 --> 00:00:22.760
where you can find a maestro
de los trajes de charro.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.660
Jorge Tello.
(festive mariachi music)

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.420
Believe it or not, this master
of the Mexican tradition

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.244
is not even from Mexico.

00:00:30.244 --> 00:00:31.621
- Guatemala.

00:00:31.621 --> 00:00:33.538
- [Narrator] Guatemala?

00:00:36.091 --> 00:00:38.135
(festive mariachi music)
(singer vocalizing)

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:39.840
(quirky music)

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.110
First things first.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.398
What's a charro?

00:00:42.398 --> 00:00:46.315
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:58.390 --> 00:00:59.960
Jorge's the guy to ask.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.552
Around these parts, he's known
as El Maestro, or The Master.

00:01:04.552 --> 00:01:08.469
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.163
As far as we're concerned,
he's lived up to the name.

00:01:13.163 --> 00:01:16.010
For the past 30 years,
Jorge and his sister Sandra

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.360
have been customizing, designing,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:18.920
and sewing these beautiful trajes

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.190
for musicians all over the world,

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:22.462
including some celebrities.

00:01:22.462 --> 00:01:26.379
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:36.113
So many, he can't even remember.

00:01:36.113 --> 00:01:38.630
(festive music)
What is it about these trajes

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.736
that sets them apart from the rest?

00:01:40.736 --> 00:01:44.653
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:56.389 --> 00:01:58.470
And you can't talk about mariachi in LA

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.773
without talking about Jorge's
neighborhood of Boyle Heights.

00:02:01.773 --> 00:02:05.690
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:17.396 --> 00:02:19.090
(festive mariachi music)

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.940
There's no place where this is
more true than mariachi plaza

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.310
where you can find mariachis
waiting for their gigs.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.740
Mariachis like Arturo Ramirez.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.738
Arturo, what do you have
to say about Maestro?

00:02:29.738 --> 00:02:33.655
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.410
And there's also Arlette Morales,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.370
whose students are also dressed by Jorge.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.287
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:52.675 --> 00:02:55.664
Jorge, any advice for any
aspiring mariachi out there?

00:02:55.664 --> 00:02:59.581
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.010
Jorge, how much longer do you see yourself

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:08.683
making trajes de charro?

00:03:08.683 --> 00:03:12.600
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:20.977 --> 00:03:22.436
How about a grito for the road?

00:03:22.436 --> 00:03:24.651
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:24.651 --> 00:03:27.818
(mariachi vocalizing)

